[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127835,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiokeratoma is a superficial vascular ectasia associated with variable overlying epidermal changes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127836,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9141/0 Angiokeratoma</p>\r\n<p>9141/0 Angiokeratoma corporis diffusum</p>\r\n<p>9141/0 Angiokeratoma of Fordyce</p>\r\n<p>9141/0 Angiokeratoma of Mibelli</p>\r\n<p>9141/0 Solitary angiokeratoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127837,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EF20.1 Angiokeratoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127838,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127839,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiokeratoma corporis diffusum (ACD); angiokeratoma of Fordyce (AF); angiokeratoma of Mibelli (AM); solitary angiokeratoma (SA)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127840,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ACD is characterized by lesions in a bathing-trunk distribution. AF occurs on the scrotum and vulva {6069899; 6019084}. AM occurs on the dorsum of fingers and toes {8993949}. SA occurs mainly on the lower limbs {8993949}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127841,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with ACD present with numerous asymptomatic pinpoint papules with minimal epidermal change. Patients with AF or AM present with single or multiple small red keratotic papules. Patients with SA present with a single keratotic (often crusted) papule or nodule.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127842,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Four clinicopathological types of angiokeratomas have been described {6018992}. ACD is associated with Fabry disease and arises in late childhood. AF most commonly occurs in adult men. AM most commonly occurs in adults, with no sex predilection. SA or angiokeratoma circumscriptum also occurs in adults, with no sex predilection.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127843,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology of sporadic angiokeratomas is unknown. In Fabry disease, the lesions seem to develop because of accumulation of globotriaosylceramide in endothelial cells as a result of &alpha;-galactosidase deficiency. ACD may also develop as a result of deficiency in other enzymes, including &alpha;-L-fucosidase {4634000}, &beta;-mannosidase {15729869}, and &alpha;-N-acetylgalactosaminidase {11251574}. Rare cases with no enzyme deficiency may be seen {6423705}.</p>\r\n<p>ACD is associated with Fabry disease, an X-linked genetic disorder of &alpha;-galactosidase deficiency.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127844,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127845,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127846,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 255,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ACD shows dilated, congested vascular channels in the papillary dermis, with minimal epidermal change. Endothelial cells, pericytes, and fibroblasts show cytoplasmic vacuoles representing lipid. AF, AM, and SA show dilated, thin-walled vascular channels in the papillary dermis associated with overlying epidermal changes consisting of acanthosis and hyperkeratosis {6018992}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>SA must be distinguished from verrucous venous malformation (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/64/259\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Verrucous venous malformation</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
